Transcription at the CRISPR locus promotes timely new spacer incorporation in vivo. The effect of transcription on new spacer acquisition was further investigated in an in vivo setting. The E. faecalis spacer acquisition cis elements (CRISPR leader and a single repeat) were grafted into the E. coli chromosome. E. faecalis Cas1 and Cas2 expression were induced, and optimal prespacers were electroporated into E. coli cells to bypass the prespacer biogenesis bottleneck. Possible run-through transcription was insulated by upstream and downstream transcriptional terminators, and a native E. coli promoter was either included in or omitted upstream of the CRISPR leader (Fig. 6a). Results showed that the spacer acquisition efficiency was reproducibly higher (\(\sim\)2.8-fold) when a promoter was present upstream of the CRISPR leader. An even stronger stimulatory effect was observed (\(\sim\)3.5-fold) when DNA replication was stalled by nalidixic acid (Fig. 6b). We attempted to further distinguish the influence of transcription from that of replication by examining the timing of new spacer acquisition using a PCR detection scheme that specifically amplified the fully incorporated new spacers (Fig. 6a). Because the replication fork passes through the CRISPR locus much less frequently than a transcribing RNA polymerase, new spacers
