# Discussion

Our work provides the temporal resolution to dissect the kinetic framework governing prespacer biogenesis, integration, and incorporation processes. It establishes the foundation to further understand spacer acquisition processes that involve specialized processing factors, such as Cas3 and Cas4 nucleases27–29,36,41–43 and reverse transcriptase44–47. The most important conceptual advance of this study is the realization that an efficient mechanism to resolve the PSC is essential to maintain robust CRISPR– Cas immunity. A persistent PSC would prevent new CRISPR RNA production and potentially weaken or halt CRISPR surveillance, whereas premature disintegration would lead to the loss of

Our work provides the temporal resolution to dissect the kinetic framework governing prespacer biogenesis, integration, and incorporation processes. It establishes the foundation to further understand spacer acquisition processes that involve specialized processing factors, such as Cas3 and Cas4 nucleases27–29,36,41–43 and reverse transcriptase44–47. The most important conceptual advance of this study is the realization that an efficient mechanism to resolve the PSC is essentially to maintain robust CRISPR– Cas immunity. A persistent PSC would prevent new CRISPR RNA production and potentially weaken or halt CRISPR surveillance, whereas premature disintegration would lead to the loss of preestablished prespacer directionality. Both scenarios are detrimental to CRISPR immunity. Here we reveal that transcription from the CRISPR leader, in combination with unscheduled DNA synthesis and the continuous spacer protection by Cas1–Cas2 efficiently and precisely resolves the PSC, allowing the completion of new spacer incorporation; the step-by-step events of spacer incorporation are shown in Fig. 7. The efficiency of such a mechanism may help rationalize the interesting observation that the Streptococcus pyogenes Type II-A CRISPR system is able to acquire new spacers almost instantaneously while a phage is injecting its DNA genome, and the updated CRISPR RNA guides Cas9 to protect the host from any subsequent infection from the same phage48. Exceptions to this theme may exist. For example, spacer acquisition in the E. coli Type I-E system requires the integration host factor protein to bend the leader, which sequesters the promoter and most likely shuts off transcription18. An integral component of the DNA replisome, DnaJ, is further required to nucleolytically process the PAM side of the prespacer to complete full integration49. The PSC resolution in this subset of the CRISPR systems may indeed be replication coupled; however, the coupling does not always take place in a timely fashion, as a small percentage of the E. coli spacers seem to have been acquired from disintegrated prespacer reintegrated in the wrong orientation37. Given the potential fitness cost of halting CRISPR RNA production during integration, we think the majority of the slower-growing prokaryotes would likely rely on the transcription-coupled mechanism to resolve the PSC in a timely fashion. Lastly, despite its great potential in cell lineage tracing and information storage applications32,33, Cas1–Cas2-mediated spacer acquisition has been difficult to reconstitute in eukaryotic cells. Our study clearly defines the bottlenecks in the spacer integration process. We hope this would lead to renewed effort to harness the power of the Cas1–Cas2 integrase.
# Methods

MethodsProtein expression and purification. The expression and purification of EfaCas1– Cas2 were done by following the protocol published in our previous work25. Briefly, the \(\operatorname{His}_{5}\) -Sumo-tagged Cas1 and Cas2 were expressed and purified separately from E. coli BL21(DE3) cells following the same protocol. Briefly, the expression cells were grown separately at \(37\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) until OD reached \({\sim}0.8\) . The culture was cooled to \(18\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , and protein expression was induced with a \(1\,\mathrm{mM}\) final concentration of IPTG. Cells were harvested from overnight cell culture by centrifuging at \(4{,}000g\) for \(20\,\mathrm{min}\) , resuspended in the lysis buffer ( \(25\,\mathrm{mM}\) HEPES, \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\) , \(500\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{NaCl}\) , \(20\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole), and lysed by sonication. After centrifugation at \(15,000\,\mathrm{r.p.m}\) . for \(40\,\mathrm{{min}}\) , the soluble fraction was loaded onto a Ni-NTA column pre-equilibrated in the lysis buffer. The unbound protein was washed from the column by three rounds of five column-volume lysis buffer, and the bound proteins were eluted with the lysis buffer supplemented with \(300\,\mathrm{mM}\) imidazole. The eluted Cas1 and Cas2 proteins were concentrated and mixed at 2:1 molar ratio to form the apo Cas1– Cas2 complex. The sumo protease was then added to cleave the \(\operatorname{His}_{6}\) -Sumo-tag from both Cas1 and Cas2. The apo Cas1–Cas2 complex with the correct stoichiometry was separated from the individual components and other impurities using size-exclusion chromatography (Superdex 200, GE Healthcare).

Introducing Cy5 label via thio-chimistry to EfaCas1–Cas2 complex. Because EfaCas2 lacks cysteine, we performed structure-guided mutagenesis and introduced an Ala19Cys substitution into EfaCas2; this residue is not conserved, and the sidechain change is not expected to interfere with EfaCas1–Cas2 function. Cas2_A19C was expressed and purified following the same procedure as for EfaCas2_WT, with the addition of a Cy5 maleimide labeling step while the protein was bound on the Ni-NTA column6. The unreacted Cy5 was removed by applying the lysis buffer without imidazole. The bound Cas2 was then eluted from the column, pooled, and concentrated. Cy5-labeled Cas2 was then complexed with Cas1 and further purified as previously described. Based on the histogram of the single-molecule fluorescence intensity, over \(70\%\) of the EfaCas1–Cas2 molecules from this labeling and purification approach were singly labeled.

Bulk integration reaction procedure for evaluating smFRET compatibility of Cy3-target and Cy5-prespacer. Bulk biochemicals was carried out to evaluate whether the attachment of Cy3 and Cy5 (both backboned labeled) on the target and prespacer affected integration reaction. The prespacer, PS(4,4), was mixed with Cas1–Cas2 in 1:1 molar ratio in a binding buffer ( \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\) NaCl, \(50\,\mathrm{mM}\) HEPES, \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\AA\) ). The polar formed complex was then reacted with \(10\,\mathrm{nM}\,\mathrm{Cy}3\) -labeled target in the molar ratio of 4:1 at \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) final concentration. \(50\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of the reaction was removed at a different time point and mixed with Tris-equilibrated phenol-chloroform, \(\mathrm{pH}\,8\) to stop the reaction. Each mixture was vortexed for \(10\,\mathrm{s}\) and spun at \(13.6\,\mathrm{rpm}\) to separate the phases. The top layer was removed and mixed with an equal volume of \(95\%\) formamide-EDTA solution for gel analysis. The samples were separated on a \(10\%\) urea-PAGE gel. The gel was scanned using appropriate lasers on Typhoon imager.

Construction of total internal reflection—based imaging system. A prism-type total internal reflection fluorescence imaging system was built around an IX73 inverted microscope (Olympus). An imaging system \(532\,\mathrm{nm}\) LS \(100\,\mathrm{mW}\) , Coherent) and a red laser (OBIS \(640\,\mathrm{nm}\) LX \(40\,\mathrm{mW}\) ) were installed on the optical table, and the incident beams were guided along the same optical path to excite donor (Cy3) and acceptor (Cy5) fluorophores, respectively. The Cy3 and Cy5 fluorescence signals were collected using a 60X water objective (UplanS Apo, \(60\times/\) 1.2 w, Olympus), filtered through a long-pass filter (BLP01-532R-25, Semrock) when the green laser was used or a notch filter (ZET635NF, Chroma) when the red laser was used. The filtered signals were then partitioned by a dichroic mirror (ZT633rdc-UF1, Chroma), and then projected onto two separate areas on an EMCCD camera (iXon, Andor), creating donor and acceptor channels for Cy3 and Cy5 signal visualization.

smFRET experimental setup. The slide cleaning, passivation and flow chamber preparation were performed by using the protocol, as described51. The microfluidic chamber was first filled with neutravidin to bind surface-biotin, and the excess unbound neutravidin was removed after 10 minutes of incubation. The biotinylated target DNA \((10{-}20\,\mathrm{pM})\) diluted in the imaging buffer \((50\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{HEPES},\,\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\) , \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) Trolox, \(0.8\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}}\) glucose, \(0.1\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}}\) glucose, \(\mathrm{pH}\,7.5\) , \(0.02\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}}\) catalase, \(10\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{MgCl}_{2}\) and \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{NaCl}\) ) was introduced into the channel. The gradual appearance of bright spots on the donor channel indicated target immobilization. Once the desired target number reached 300–400, the channel was flushed with imaging buffer to remove unbound DNA. For the actual smFRET experiments, Cas1–Cas2 and prespacer were preassembled in 1:1 molar ratio at \(200{-}500\,\mathrm{nM}\) concentration for 20 minutes to assemble the binary complex, in a buffer containing \(50\,\mathrm{mM}\) HEPES \(\mathrm{(pH}\,7.5\AA)\) and \(100\,\mathrm{mM}\,\mathrm{NaCl}\) .

smFRET recording of prespacer integration under native conditions. The preassembled Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4) was diluted in the desired concentration \((1{-}50\,\mathrm{nM})\) using imaging buffer just prior to use in an experiment. The diluted solution was then loaded to the buffer reservoir mounted on one end of the flow

smFRET recording of prespacer integration under native conditions. The preassembled Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4) was diluted in the desired concentration \((1{-}50\,\mathrm{nM})\) using imaging buffer just prior to use in an experiment. The diluted solution was then loaded to the buffer reservoir mounted on one end of the flow cell. We recorded movies of varying lengths for different purposes. For example, to capture the initial interaction of Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4) upon target encounter (pre-steady state), we recorded a relatively long movie lasting for about \(5\,\mathrm{min}\) . The movie recording started 10 s before the introduction of \(50{-}80\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) Cas1– Cas2–PS(4,4), drawn from the reservoir into the chamber by applying negative pressure from the opposite opening. The movie recording continued until the photobleaching of Cy3. This way, we were able to capture multiple cycles and/ or different modes of interactions. After the system attained a steady state, up to four additional 2- to 3-min movies were also recorded to capture the steady-state behavior. At last, 25 short movies (12 frames each) were also recorded for capturing the distribution of molecules in different FRET states. All movies were analyzed by using an analysis software package downloaded from the CPLC webpage (https:// cplc.illinois.edu/software). Long movies were converted to FRET trajectories for single molecules using the movie analysis program. The first ten frames of each trajectory were combined to build the FRET efficiency histogram. We refer to this histogram as the 'native-state histogram', as Cas1–Cas2 is still interacting with the prespacer and target. A MATLAB graphical user interface was created (which can be made available upon request) to further process smFRET traces for vbFRET, dwell time and TDP plot analyses.

SDS denaturation procedure for the purpose of distinguishing binding from integration. Two-percent SDS in the imaging solution \(\mathrm{50\,mM}\) Tris-HCl, pH 6.8, \(2\,\mathrm{mM}\) Trolox, \(0.8\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}}\) glucose, \(0.1\,\mathrm{mg\,ml^{-1}}\) glucose oxidase, \(0.02\,\mathrm{mg}\) \(\mathrm{ml^{-1}}\) catalase, \(\mathrm{NaCl}\;100\,\mathrm{mM}\) , \(2\%\) SDS added last) was introduced during movie recording to denature and remove Cas1–Cas2. Unreacted prespacer DNA was removed by washing with SDS, whereas integrated prespacer remained. This washing step was typically applied toward the end of the native-state movie recording. The transition from the native to denaturing FRET state allowed us to define the integration status of a given single molecule. Twenty-five short movies with the SDS-wash step incorporated were recorded at different areas and combined to generate the denaturing-state histogram.

Transition density plot analysis. smFRET traces containing binding and upholding transitions (Fig. 2a,c,e) or native to SDS-denatured transitions (Extended Data Figs. 6a and 7b,c) were hand-picked and fed to vbFRET software52 for finding hidden FRET states. The resulting 'idealized traces' were analyzed by an in-house MATLAB script to generate TDP.

Dwell-time analysis to determine association rate constant \((k_{\mathrm{on}})\) , dissociation rate constant \((k_{\mathrm{off}})\) , reverse half-integration rate \((k^{-1}_{\mathrm{half}})\) and reverse full-integration rate \((k^{-1}_{\mathrm{full}})\) . Before carrying out \(k_{\mathrm{on}}\) and \(k_{\mathrm{off}}\) analyses, smFRET traces were subjected to vbFRET hidden state analyzer. Once the idealized traces were generated by vbFRET, the rest of the procedure was semiautomated via a custom-made MATLAB graphical user interface. To determine \(k_{\mathrm{on}}\) (which is based on off time or \(\tau_{\mathrm{off}})\) , off-state dwell times were collected from at least 100 idealized traces (Fig. 2a,c,e) programmatically and plotted on a histogram. By fitting the gamma function, average \(\tau_{\mathrm{off}}\) was determined, the inverse of which gives binding rate. The binding rate for each concentration of Cas1–Cas2–PS(4ddC,4ddC) was determined from the program and plotted against the corresponding concentration. The binding rate versus concentration was linearly fitted, the slope of which gives \(k_{\mathrm{on}}\) (Fig. 2d). To find \(k_{\mathrm{off}}\) , on-state dwell times \((\tau_{\mathrm{on}})\) were collected. Even though Cas1–Cas2–PS(4ddC,4ddC) binding occurs in two orientations, we took both as on state in our analysis of \(k_{\mathrm{off}}\) . The rest of the procedure for finding \(\tau_{\mathrm{on}}\) was similar to that of \(\tau_{\mathrm{off}}\) . The inverse of \(\tau_{\mathrm{on}}\) gives \(k_{\mathrm{off}}\) (Fig. 2f). The \(k_{\mathrm{off}}\) was found from multiple concentrations and reported as mean \(\pm\;\mathbf{s}.\mathbf{e}.\mathbf{m}\) .

For the determination of \(k^{-1}_{\mathrm{half}}\) and \(k^{-1}_{\mathrm{full}}\) , experiments were carried out at a power laser power of \(3{-}5\,\mathrm{mW}.\) . To compensate for the signal loss due to low laser power, movies were recorded at a speed of \(2.5{-}5\,\mathrm{Hz}\) . The challenge with these experiments was to record a long movie for \({\sim}20\,\mathrm{min}\) , the doubling time for \(E\) . coli. Therefore, the stability of Cy3 and Cy5 were tested beforehand (Extended Data Fig. 7), which showed remarkable durability against photobleaching and blinking. The procedure is both types of the experiment \((k^{-1}_{\mathrm{half}}\,{=}\,\tau_{\mathrm{off}})\) is to introduce Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4ddC) or Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4) in the target-containing channel and allow integration for \(4{-}5\,\mathrm{min}\) . While integration was ongoing, excess Cas1– Cas2–prespacer was washed out by gentle imaging buffer flow \((30{-}40\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\,\mathrm{min^{-1}})\) . Once \(30{-}40\,\upmu\mathrm{l}\) of the buffer flowed through the channel (volume of the channel is \({\sim}10\,\upmu\mathrm{l})\) , the buffer flow was stopped. The surviving molecules on the imaging area after the flow are in an integrated state. The time it takes for them to dissociate from the target determines the rate of disintegration. The movie recording began \(10\,\mathrm{s}\) before Cas1–Cas2–prespacer flow and continued for next \(20{-}25\,\mathrm{min}\) under continuous laser exposure.

To determine \(k^{-1}_{\mathrm{half}}\) Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4ddC) was flowed through the channel, and the above procedure was followed to collect movies. Then, the movies were analyzed to generate smFRET traces. The traces were marked at the time when imaging buffer flowed in, for example, \(267\,\mathrm{s}\) in Fig. 3a. The time point at which the Cy5 signal disappeared was noted (time), which is due to detachment of Cas1–Cas2–PS(4,4ddC) from the target. Tracing back along the FRET efficiency curve, the point of integration (time,) was also noted. The difference between these two time points is the half-integration dwell time. As many as five independent
